#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Vitulus Master Controller Node.

Calendar-driven scheduler with sleep coordination. The main manager that
drives the robot: it decides *when* to mow (calendar), starts/resumes
programs via the planner, watches how a program ended (via mower_smach
``stop_reason``) and puts the robot to sleep / wakes it accordingly.

Programs describe *what* to mow; the calendar describes *when*.
FSM ticks at 1 Hz. Persistent state in ~/.vitulus/scheduler_state.json.

Weather / battery / temperature checks live in mower_smach (PreStartCheck +
concurrent monitors). They are reported here through ``/mower_smach/stop_reason``
and this node reacts to them:

  - succeeded                  -> clear interrupt, idle / wait-for-dock
  - interrupted:battery        -> charge sleep, then resume
  - interrupted:weather        -> sleep weather_sleep_duration_h, then resume
  - interrupted:temp           -> sleep temp_sleep_duration_h, then resume
  - interrupted:temp_spike     -> stay awake temp_spike_wait_min, then resume
  - interrupted:temp_sensor    -> report it, no resume: the blade has no protection
  - rejected:rain|recent_rain|forecast (at scheduled start)
                               -> treat like weather interrupt (keep & retry)
  - rejected:battery           -> charge sleep, then resume
  - critical:*                 -> sleep critical_error_sleep_duration_h
  - terminal:* / stopped:*     -> clear interrupt, no auto-sleep (manual/terminal)

Recovery is capped so the robot always wakes before the next scheduled
program. A global allowed time-window ("don't mow at night") gates all
calendar starts/resumes; it does not concern a manual Run or the agent. Its
defaults are in the yaml, the owner's setting is in the state file
("allowed_window", /scheduler/set_window, in force at once).

Activation: one persisted switch (scheduler_state.json "automation_enabled",
default OFF; /scheduler/set_enabled topic + service, /scheduler/enabled
latched). OFF = state DISABLED: nothing is started, nothing sleeps.

A calendar slot goes:  pending -> run (persisted, "run" in the state file) ->
really started (last_run_by_event, only when the mission reports it left the
start checks) -> finished. A slot nobody could start stays pending for
catchup_max_h, then it is reported as missed (see _scan_slots). A run lives
until it finishes, whatever waits it goes through: battery, rain, temperature,
window end, a crash; every wait ends in a resume at the mission's checkpoint.
Failures are capped (see _run_cap_reached) and reported on /scheduler/report.
"""

